/* ─── CART DRAWER + AVAILABLE OFFERS PANEL — moved to jn-header.liquid, shared by
   every page, instead of living here as this page's own independent copy
   (jiineva-product.liquid and jiineva-home.liquid each had their own too — all
   three are now unified into one). ── */

/* ─── QUICK-ADD SIZE SHEET — opened from a product card's + / bag icon when
   that product has more than one variant (product.variants.size > 1, read
   off data-variant-count with zero extra fetch). A single-variant product
   skips this entirely and adds directly, exactly as before.

   REDESIGNED. What was wrong with the previous version, and what each
   change is actually for:

   1. The sizes were not recognisable as controls. .jnc-qa-option-btn had
      `border: none; background: none`, so "1  2  3" rendered as three
      loose words with no box, no edge and nothing to aim at — the single
      biggest reason the sheet read as unfinished. They are now real
      bordered chips with a visible resting state, a hover, a filled
      selected state and a distinct struck-through sold-out state.
   2. Those chips are 48x48 minimum. The old ones were 27x24, well under
      the ~44px minimum comfortable touch target, so on a phone they were
      genuinely hard to hit.
   3. The footer now sticks to the bottom of the sheet instead of sitting
      at the end of a scrolling column, which is why BUY NOW was previously
      cut off below the fold with a scrollbar running down the side. The
      body scrolls between a pinned header and a pinned footer, so the
      primary action is always reachable without scrolling.
   4. A SIZE GUIDE trigger now sits opposite the SIZE label, answering
      "what if I don't know my size" without leaving the sheet. It opens
      the same measurement panel the PDP uses, with the same content —
      see the MEASUREMENT GUIDE PANEL block further down.

   Shape is unchanged and deliberate: bottom sheet on mobile, centred
   modal on desktop — the conventional, expected form for a quick
   pick-a-size step, and the same language jnp-drawer already uses. ─── */
.jnc-qa-overlay {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2);
  backdrop-filter: blur(20px);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(20px);
  z-index: 209;
  display: none;
}
.jnc-qa-overlay.jnp-open { display: block; }
.jnc-qa-sheet {
  position: fixed;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: -100%;
  width: 100%;
  /* dvh so the sheet's own bottom edge stays inside the real visible
     screen on a phone rather than the inflated 100vh figure, the same fix
     .jnp-drawer-panel and .jnp-detail-overlay-panel already carry. */
  max-height: 85vh;
  max-height: 85dvh;
  background: #FFFFFF;
  z-index: 210;
  transition: bottom 0.3s ease;
  border-radius: 16px 16px 0 0;
  /* Column layout is what lets the header and footer pin while only the
     middle scrolls — the old sheet was a single overflow-y:auto block, so
     everything including the buttons scrolled together. */
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  overflow: hidden;
}
.jnc-qa-sheet.jnp-open { bottom: 0; }
/* Grab handle — the standard affordance that says "this is a sheet you can
   dismiss". Mobile only; hidden on desktop where the sheet is a modal. */
.jnc-qa-grabber {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  width: 36px;
  height: 4px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: #d8d8d8;
  margin: 8px auto 0;
}
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .jnc-qa-sheet {
    left: 50%;
    right: auto;
    bottom: auto;
    top: 50%;
    width: 440px;
    max-width: calc(100vw - 48px);
    transform: translate(-50%, -45%);
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
    border-radius: 4px;
    /* Desktop shows the whole modal in one frame — no inner scrolling.
       This was 80vh, which is plenty at a full-height window but clamps
       below the ~450px the content needs once the usable viewport drops
       under about 560px. That happens more easily than it sounds: Windows
       display scaling at 125-175% shrinks the CSS viewport well below the
       screen's pixel height, and browser chrome takes another slice. When
       the clamp bit, the flex column had to take the space out of the one
       flexible child — .jnc-qa-scroll — which is why the size chips
       disappeared behind the footer behind a scrollbar.

       92vh so the modal keeps a small breathing margin against the window
       edges while giving the content virtually all of the height it asks
       for, and overflow-y:auto on the sheet ITSELF (rather than hidden,
       from the base rule) so that in the extreme case of a product with
       far more sizes than fit any screen, the whole modal scrolls as one
       piece instead of trapping the options in a cramped inner pane. */
    max-height: 92vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }
  .jnc-qa-sheet.jnp-open {
    opacity: 1;
    pointer-events: auto;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    /* THE ACTUAL CAUSE of the desktop modal being scrollable, and it was
       here from the start. `.jnc-qa-sheet.jnp-open { bottom: 0 }` further
       up is what slides the MOBILE sheet into view, and it outranks the
       desktop `bottom: auto` above on specificity (0,2,0 beats 0,1,0), so
       it kept applying here too. An element with both `top: 50%` and
       `bottom: 0` is stretched between those two edges — which pinned the
       modal to exactly half the window's height on every desktop screen,
       regardless of how much content it held. At a 900px-tall window that
       is 450px, which just happened to be almost exactly what the content
       needed, so it looked fine; any shorter window and the flex column
       had to claw the difference back out of .jnc-qa-scroll, which is why
       the size chips vanished behind a scrollbar.

       Resetting bottom to auto lets the modal do what it should: size to
       its own content and sit centred. */
    bottom: auto;
  }
  .jnc-qa-grabber { display: none; }
}
.jnc-qa-header {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 16px 24px;
